Pages touched: [[sources/fu2019-heavy-metals-human-metabolism]], [[sources/khan2015-heavy-metals-food-plants-bioaccumulation]], [[sources/gomez-arroyo2017-dandelion-biomonitor-mexico-city]], [[sources/meng2023-electrochemical-sensors-food-heavy-metals]], [[sources/sharma2023-phytoremediation-heavy-metal-soil]], [[sources/si2024-nanomaterial-detection-heavy-metals-food]], [[sources/kalicharan2025-heavy-metals-pet-food-south-africa]], [[sources/ali2024-leek-microbial-metals-iraq]], [[sources/guan2014-soil-heavy-metal-food-safety-china]]
Notes: 9 source pages ingested from Papers Cube Manual Fetch. fu2019 (B-tier): human metabolism review covering As/Pb/Cd/Hg/Ni mechanisms; useful for health pages but over-emphasizes drinking water vs dietary exposure. khan2015 (A-tier): comprehensive food plant bioaccumulation review with transfer factor data (leaf > root > grain hierarchy; Cd TF 1–10 in leafy veg; As into rice via phosphate transporters); essential supply-chain and ingredient-page reference. gomez-arroyo2017 (A-tier): dandelion biomonitor in Mexico City metropolitan area; higher metals in dry season; Al dominant at Altzomoni; V dominant at urban CCA; useful for atmospheric deposition and seasonal variance context. meng2023 (B-tier, CC BY): electrochemical sensor review in Food Control; covers SWASV/DPASV, AuNP/graphene/Fe₃O₄ composites; LOD benchmarks for Pb/Cd/As in food matrices vs WHO/China limits. sharma2023 (B-tier, CC BY): Frontiers phytoremediation review covering phytoextraction, phytostabilization, phytovolatilization, rhizofiltration; MT/PC/HMA molecular mechanisms; relevant to mitigation/agronomic page. si2024 (B-tier, CC BY): Frontiers Chemistry mini-review adding colorimetric (AuNP-aptamer) and fluorescent (quantum dot) detection alongside electrochemical; complementary to meng2023, more recent. kalicharan2025 (C-tier): pet food ICP-OES study South Africa (n=31); classified C-tier (not human food); mean dog food: As 7.47, Cd 6.23, Pb 3.99, Hg 0.47, Ni 16.72 mg/kg; red meat > poultry > fish for most metals. ali2024 (B-tier, conference): leek contamination Iraq (n=100); soil Ni 94–101, Cu 44–62, Pb 20–21 µg/g; highest Zn in plant tissue 28.41 ppm summer; limited HMT&C relevance but geographic variance context. guan2014 (C-tier): 3-page Chinese conference paper, qualitative background only, no original data. SKIPS (per task instructions): pasumpon2021, pollard2016, article (2) copy (wastewater), Heavy Metals in Infant Clothing, mathew2015. New-page proposals: none (all relevant destinations are existing pages).

## [2026-05-15] ingest | papers-cube-new-tier1 — 3 Tier 1 baby food/infant formula source pages created
Pages touched: [[sources/sadiq2021-baby-rice-cereal-bioaccessibility-speciation]], [[sources/parker2022-baby-food-risk-assessment-as-cd-hg-pb]], [[sources/gardener2019-pb-cd-us-infant-formula-baby-foods]]
Notes: All three are directly relevant to HMT&C infant food certification. sadiq2021 is the strongest: simultaneous HPLC-ICP-MS speciation of As, Cr, Se in bio-accessible fractions of 3 Canadian baby rice cereals; 100% As bio-accessibility, all As(V) (inorganic), bio-accessible As exceeds FDA 100 ppb limit in all three brands; Cr(VI) found at 15–50% of bio-accessible Cr. parker2022 is a US risk assessment across 4 ingredient categories (n=36 products, Pittsburgh PA); grain products (rice-dominant) drive all elevated As and Pb signals; Hg not detected in any sample; Cd and Hg not associated with risk. gardener2019 (Clean Label Project) is the largest dataset (n=564 US baby foods and formulas); Pb in 37%, Cd in 57% of samples; 22% of infant formulas exceed Prop 65 Pb; 14% exceed WHO PTMI Cd; organic certification provides no protection; rice is the key ingredient driver for both metals. New-page proposals: none — all products and ingredients covered by existing pages.


